From: Juan Pablo Lassala <jpablolassala@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 14:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] toplevel: Size a packed panel by its surface's scale

A panel that isn't expanded asks for its applets' width times the widget's scale
factor, and the toplevel divides that by the scale it keeps. It keeps the one it
started with, and updates it only when the monitors change. A Wayland panel
started with scale 1 and then placed on an output of a higher scale, as on a
desktop with outputs of different scales, is never told: it doubled its width at
a scale of 1.5 or 2, leaving the second half empty.

Divide by the widget's scale factor, the one that was multiplied by.
---
 mate-panel/panel-toplevel.c | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/mate-panel/panel-toplevel.c b/mate-panel/panel-toplevel.c
--- a/mate-panel/panel-toplevel.c
+++ b/mate-panel/panel-toplevel.c
@@ -2430,8 +2430,11 @@
 	int total_size;
 	int full_hints;
 
-	/* Scale down the size so that the panel only takes what it needs for the applets it has. */
-	total_size = non_panel_widget_size + (requisition_size / toplevel->priv->scale);
+	/* Scale down the size so that the panel only takes what it needs for the applets it has.
+	 * The surface's scale, not the one the panel started with: it changes when the surface
+	 * goes to an output of another scale, and no monitor has changed for that. */
+	total_size = non_panel_widget_size +
+		     (requisition_size / gtk_widget_get_scale_factor (GTK_WIDGET (toplevel)));
 
 	nb_size_hints = toplevel->priv->panel_widget->nb_applets_size_hints;
 	if (nb_size_hints <= 0)
